[[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07561","Ibid., vol. 2, pp. 321–322.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07562","al-Farq bayna al-Firaq, p. 124.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07563","al-Munya wa-l-Amal, p. 29.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07564","al-Milal wa-l-Niḥal, vol. 1, p. 57.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07565","al-Naẓẓām’s own words support this interpretation of the motion he calls iʿtimād. As quoted in al-Ashʿarī’s Maqālāt (vol. 2, p. 254), he says that “when a body moves from one place to another, the motion occurs in the first place. It is its iʿtimād, which brings about its being in the second place; and its being in the second place is the body’s motion in that second place.”↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07566","By saying “in language,” he means that rest is only a conventional term and is not something real in itself.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07567","Maqālāt al-Islāmiyyīn, vol. 2, p. 324.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07568","al-Ashʿarī, Maqālāt al-Islāmiyyīn, vol. 2, p. 325.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07569","Ibid., p. 232.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07570","Ibid., p. 327.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07571","Liṭāf is the plural of laṭīf. Here laṭīf means the opposite of dense: a body so lacking in density that it cannot be seen with the naked eye or detected by the other outward senses.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07572","Ibid., p. 347.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07573","Ibid., pp. 333–334.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07574","Ibid., p. 331.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07575","al-Milal wa-l-Niḥal, vol. 1, p. 57.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07576","The āfa of a thing means anything that harms it or acts against it.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07577","al-Ashʿarī, Maqālāt al-Islāmiyyīn, vol. 2, p. 339.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07578","Ibid., same page.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07579","Ibid., p. 369.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07580","We should remember here al-Naẓẓām’s theory that the world was created all at once, which he supported with his theory of “latency and manifestation.” There too, he made motion the starting point and basic principle of the theory.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07581","de Boer, The History of Philosophy in Islam, p. 78.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07582","al-Shahrastānī, al-Milal, vol. 1, p. 48.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07583","See ʿAlī Sāmī al-Nashshār, The Rise of Philosophical Thought in Islam, vol. 1, p. 519.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07584","Ibid., p. 578; and Aḥmad Amīn, Ḍuḥā al-Islām, vol. 3, p. 106 ff.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07585","Albert Naṣrī Nādir, The Muʿtazila, Beirut, 1950, p. 20.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07586","al-Baghdādī, al-Farq bayna al-Firaq, p. 100.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07587","al-Ashʿarī, al-Maqālāt, vol. 2, pp. 456–457.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07588","Ibn Abī al-Ḥadīd, Sharḥ Nahj al-Balāgha, vol. 1, p. 66.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07589","al-Ashʿarī, al-Maqālāt, vol. 2, pp. 461–462.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07590","al-Ashʿarī, al-Maqālāt, vol. 2, p. 466.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07591","Ibid., p. 467.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07592","al-Shahrastānī, al-Milal, vol. 1, p. 56.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07593","al-Ashʿarī, al-Maqālāt, vol. 1, p. 200.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07594","al-Baghdādī, al-Farq bayna al-Firaq, p. 115.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07595","Ibid., p. 114.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07596","al-Shahrastānī, al-Milal, vol. 1, p. 57.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07597","For clarification of this issue, see al-Khayyāṭ, al-Intiṣār, p. 85.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07598","Summarized from al-Shahrastānī, Nihāyat al-Iqdām, p. 397, and al-Khayyāṭ, al-Intiṣār, p. 85.↩︎"],[7,"en","Footnotes","passage-en-07599","See also Nihāyat 